## Exclusive Write Access

A mutable borrow (`&mut T`) guarantees exclusive access — you're the only one who can read or write the value.

### Single &mut T

```xiom
var data = Vec[Int]();
data.push(1);

let m = &mut data;
m.push(2);
// let m2 = &mut data;  // ERROR: only one &mut at a time
```

### &mut Prevents All Other Access

```xiom
var data = Vec[Int]();
data.push(1);
let m = &mut data;
// let r = &data;       // ERROR: cannot shared-borrow
// let n = data.len();  // ERROR: cannot use original
// data.push(2);        // ERROR: cannot use original
m.push(3);  // Only m can access data
```

### Mutable Borrow Through Functions

```xiom
fn increment_all(v: &mut Vec[Int]) -> Unit {
  var i = 0;
  while i < v.len() {
    v[i] = v[i] + 1;
    i = i + 1;
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  var data = Vec[Int]();
  data.push(1); data.push(2);
  increment_all(&mut data);
  // data is [2, 3]
  return data[0];
}
```

### Sequential Mutable Borrows

You CAN have multiple mutable borrows sequentially (one after another):

```xiom
var data = Vec[Int]();
data.push(1);
{
  let m1 = &mut data;
  m1.push(2);
}  // m1 ends
{
  let m2 = &mut data;
  m2.push(3);
}  // m2 ends
```

### Tips for &mut T

- Keep mutable borrows as short as possible
- Use scoped blocks `{ }` to limit borrow duration
- Consider restructuring code to avoid long borrows
- When stuck, `.clone()` the data

